course: dictionary
lesson: 4522
title: Headlines and the news
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A headline is the big title printed above a news story. It tells you what the story is about in a few short, bold words. «A headline is the title of a news story; it is short, bold and grabs attention.» Use the words around it to check what it means.
explain: Headlines are written to grab you, so they can be exciting or shocking. Read the story underneath before you believe it or share it. «Headlines are written to grab you, so read the story underneath; a shocking post that is on no trusted news site is probably fake.»
explain: Context helps with the phrase «made headlines». In «The Mona Lisa was stolen from the Louvre in 1911 and found two years later, which made headlines everywhere», the clues are stolen and found. So made headlines must mean became big news everywhere, not that the words were printed in large letters.
fact: A headline is the title printed in large letters above a story in a newspaper or on a news website.
fact: «The headlines» means the most important news stories of the day.
fact: As a verb, headline means to be the main act at a concert or show.
fact: Headlines are often short and in the present tense, like «Dog Saves Boy».
fact: A headline can be written to shock or to make you click, so always read the whole story.
exercise: «A headline is the title of a news story; it is short, bold and grabs attention.» What is a headline? | The last paragraph of a news story | A list of the days of the week | *The title printed in large letters above a news story | A photo with a long caption
exercise: «The Mona Lisa ... made headlines everywhere.» What does made headlines mean here? | It was printed in very small letters | It was sold for a small amount of money | *Became big news everywhere | It was painted on every wall
exercise: Ella reads, «The band will headline the concert tonight.» What does headline mean in this sentence? | To print a list of the news | *To be the main act at a show | To say a word out loud twice | To hide a story from readers
test: «Headlines are written to grab you, so read the story underneath.» Why should you read the whole story before you believe a headline? | To find the photo caption | To check it is not fake or misleading | To count the number of words in the headline | *To find out which newspaper printed it
test: Ahmed sees «The headlines» on a news website. What does this phrase mean? | A list of songs in a music shop | An old newspaper from last year | *The most important news stories of the day | The top layer of a cake
exam: Which description best matches a headline? | A picture that shows the news without any words | A quiz question printed at the end of a newspaper | A long story with many paragraphs and no title | *A short, bold, catchy title for a news story
